            {
              "id": "24030b03-a94b-357b-b1b2-b6aa3e315014",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of non-cryptographic or weak random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable values, which can be exploited by an attacker. The `rand()` function in C++ is a non-cryptographic random number generator, which means it is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is `ha[i] = rand() % 3`. This line of code is generating a random number between 0 and 2. However, because it uses `rand()`, the generated number is not cryptographically secure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use the `random_device` class from the `<random>` library, which is designed to produce non-deterministic random numbers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, 2);\n\nha[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister engine (`mt19937`), which is a high-quality pseudo-random number generator. Then, `uniform_int_distribution` is used to ensure that the generated numbers are evenly distributed between 0 and 2.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
