            {
              "id": "90745813-98e2-3788-9f8e-92a7259105e7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\nThe `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the specified environment variable is not found, the function returns a null pointer. If the returned pointer is directly used without validation, it can lead to null pointer dereferencing issues. Moreover, if an attacker can control the environment variable, they can manipulate the program's behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n- Avoid using `getenv` function if possible. Use secure alternatives that provide better control over the environment.\n- Always validate the returned pointer from `getenv` function. Never assume it will always return a valid pointer.\n- Be aware of the potential for an attacker to control environment variables. Consider all environment variables as untrusted input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\nchar* env = getenv(\"ROCBLAS_BENCH_FREQ_ALL\");\nif (env != NULL) {\n    env2 = env;\n} else {\n    // Handle the error or set a default value\n    env2 = \"default_value\";\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if the `getenv` function returns a null pointer. If it does, we handle the error or set a default value. If it doesn't, we assign the returned pointer to `env2`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `cstdlib` (for `getenv` function)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8e25c0fa-3372-3f15-9ac5-1773e1e8f59a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\nThe `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the specified environment variable is not found, the function returns a null pointer. If the returned pointer is directly used without validation, it can lead to null pointer dereferencing issues. Moreover, if an attacker can control the environment variable, they can manipulate the program's behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n- Avoid using `getenv` function if possible. Use secure alternatives that provide better control over the environment.\n- Always validate the returned pointer from `getenv` function. Never assume it will always return a valid pointer.\n- Be aware of the potential for an attacker to control environment variables. Consider all environment variables as untrusted input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\nchar* env = getenv(\"ROCBLAS_BENCH_FREQ_ALL\");\nif (env != NULL) {\n    env2 = env;\n} else {\n    // Handle the error or set a default value\n    env2 = \"default_value\";\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if the `getenv` function returns a null pointer. If it does, we handle the error or set a default value. If it doesn't, we assign the returned pointer to `env2`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `cstdlib` (for `getenv` function)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
